There are several ways to run JavaScript in a Playwright page, depending on whether you want to execute a one-off script, handle a DOM element, initialize a global variable, or bridge communication between Node.js and the browser.

Here are the four primary methods:

---

### 1. `page.evaluate()` (Most Common)
Use this when you want to run a script and get a **serializable value** back (like a string, number, or plain JSON object).

The code inside the function runs in the **browser context**, not the Node.js context.

```javascript
// Example: Get the page title
const title = await page.evaluate(() => {
  return document.title; 
});
console.log(title);

// Example: Pass data from Node.js into the browser script
const username = 'JohnDoe';
const result = await page.evaluate((name) => {
  // 'name' is accessible here because we passed it as an argument
  document.body.style.backgroundColor = 'red';
  return `Hello, ${name}`;
}, username);
```

---

### 2. `page.evaluateHandle()`
Use this when you want to interact with a **DOM element** or a complex object that cannot be easily serialized. Instead of returning the object directly, it returns a **Handle** (a reference to the object in the browser).

```javascript
// Get a handle to the first H1 element
const h1Handle = await page.evaluateHandle(() => {
  return document.querySelector('h1');
});

// You can then use that handle to perform actions
await h1Handle.evaluate((el) => {
  el.innerText = 'New Heading Content';
});
```

---

### 3. `page.addInitScript()`
Use this to run JavaScript **before** any other scripts on the page load. This is perfect for mocking global variables, polyfills, or overriding `window` properties.

```javascript
// This will run before the page even starts loading its own JS
await page.addInitScript(() => {
  window.isTestEnvironment = true;
  window.fetch = (url) => {
    console.log(`Mocking fetch to: ${url}`);
    return Promise.resolve({ json: () => ({ data: 'mocked' }) });
  };
});

await page.goto('https://example.com');
```

---

### 4. `page.exposeFunction()`
Use this if you need to call a **Node.js function** from inside the browser's JavaScript. This "bridges" the two environments.

```javascript
// Define a function in your Node.js environment
await page.exposeFunction('logToNode', (message) => {
  console.log(`Message from browser: ${message}`);
});

// Now, inside page.evaluate, you can call it like a normal global function
await page.evaluate(() => {
  // This calls the Node.js function defined above
  window.logToNode('Hello from the browser console!');
});
```

---

### Summary Table: Which one to use?

| Method | Use Case | Execution Timing | Return Type |
| :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- |
| **`evaluate`** | Get text, click a button, or change a style. | On demand | JSON-serializable data |
| **`evaluateHandle`** | Get a reference to a DOM element to use later. | On demand | A Playwright Handle |
| **`addInitScript`** | Mocking APIs, setting global flags, polyfills. | Before page load | None |
| **`exposeFunction`**| Triggering Node.js logic (e.g., DB writes) from the browser. | On demand | None (Executes Node code) |

### ⚠️ Important Note: Scope
Remember that the code inside `evaluate` **cannot** access your local Node.js variables directly. You must pass them as arguments:

```javascript
// ❌ THIS WILL FAIL (variable 'myVar' is not defined in the browser)
await page.evaluate(() => console.log(myVar));

// ✅ THIS WORKS (variable 'myVar' is passed into the browser scope)
await page.evaluate((val) => console.log(val), myVar);
```

Retrieving the `navigator` object as JSON is a bit tricky because the `navigator` object in the browser contains **functions, circular references, and non-enumerable properties**. 

If you try to simply return `navigator` inside `page.evaluate()`, Playwright will return an empty object `{}` because it cannot serialize functions or complex internal browser objects into JSON.

Here are the three best ways to do this, depending on your needs.

### Method 1: The "Specific Keys" Way (Recommended)
This is the most reliable method. Since you usually only need specific pieces of information (like `userAgent`, `platform`, or `languages`), you should explicitly map them.

```javascript
const navData = await page.evaluate(() => {
  return {
    userAgent: navigator.userAgent,
    platform: navigator.platform,
    language: navigator.language,
    hardwareConcurrency: navigator.hardwareConcurrency,
    deviceMemory: navigator.deviceMemory,
    webdriver: navigator.webdriver,
    cookiesEnabled: navigator.cookiesEnabled,
  };
});

console.log(navData);
```

---

### Method 2: The "Dynamic" Way (Get all serializable properties)
If you want to grab every property that is a simple value (string, number, boolean) without listing them manually, you can loop through the keys and filter out functions and objects.

```javascript
const navData = await page.evaluate(() => {
  const data = {};
  for (let key in navigator) {
    const value = navigator[key];
    // Only include properties that are not functions and not objects
    // (to avoid circular references and non-serializable types)
    if (typeof value !== 'function' && typeof value !== 'object') {
      data[key] = value;
    }
  }
  return data;
});

console.log(navData);
```

---

### Method 3: The "Deep Clone" Way (For Nested Objects)
Some parts of the navigator (like `navigator.permissions`) are objects. If you need to get those, you have to manually pick them out, as standard JSON serialization will still fail on them.

```javascript
const navData = await page.evaluate(() => {
  return {
    userAgent: navigator.userAgent,
    // Manually handle nested objects
    permissions: Array.from(navigator.permissions.queryRequested()).map(p => ({
      name: p.name,
      state: p.state
    })),
  };
});
```

---

### Summary: Why can't I just do `return navigator`?
If you try this:
```javascript
// ❌ THIS WILL RETURN {}
const data = await page.evaluate(() => navigator); 
```
**It fails because:**
1. **Non-enumerable properties:** Many properties on `navigator` are hidden from standard loops.
2. **Functions:** `navigator` contains methods like `getUserMedia()`. JSON cannot store functions.
3. **Security:** Browsers restrict access to certain `navigator` properties to prevent fingerprinting, and Playwright's serialization layer respects these restrictions.

**Verdict:** Use **Method 1** if you know what you need (cleanest and fastest). Use **Method 2** if you are debugging and want to see "everything" available.
